    </div>

    <div class="guide-content">

      <!-- MOBILE DEVICES -->
      <div class="content-card" id="c1-mobile">
        <h2><div class="section-icon icon-blue">📱</div> Domain 1: Mobile Devices (15%)</h2>
        <p>This domain covers laptop hardware, display types, mobile device types, connection types, and accessories.</p>

        <h3>Laptop Hardware Components</h3>
        <p>Laptops use <strong>proprietary form factors</strong> — components are specific to each model. Key things to know:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>RAM:</strong> Laptops use <span class="term">SO-DIMM</span> (Small Outline DIMM) — smaller than desktop DIMMs</li>
          <li><strong>Storage:</strong> 2.5" SATA drives, M.2 NVMe SSDs, or eMMC (soldered flash)</li>
          <li><strong>CPU:</strong> Mobile CPUs are soldered (<span class="term">BGA</span> — Ball Grid Array) — cannot be replaced</li>
          <li><strong>Battery:</strong> Lithium-Ion or Lithium-Polymer; follow disposal regulations</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="must-know">
          <div class="must-know-header">⚠️ MUST KNOW — Display Types</div>
          <p>• <strong>LCD (IPS):</strong> Best color accuracy, widest viewing angles — common in quality laptops</p>
          <p>• <strong>LCD (TN):</strong> Fastest response time, poor viewing angles, cheapest</p>
          <p>• <strong>LCD (VA):</strong> Best contrast ratios, middle ground between IPS and TN</p>
          <p>• <strong>OLED:</strong> True blacks, most vibrant colors, risk of burn-in, used in premium phones/laptops</p>
          <p>• <strong>Backlight:</strong> Modern LCDs use <span class="term">LED backlight</span>. Older used CCFL (fluorescent)</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Display Connections (Laptops)</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Port</th><th>Key Facts</th></tr>
          <tr><td>HDMI</td><td>Carries audio + video. Most common. Multiple versions (1.4, 2.0, 2.1)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>DisplayPort</td><td>Higher bandwidth than HDMI, daisy-chaining supported, common on workstations</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Thunderbolt 3/4</td><td>USB-C connector, up to 40 Gbps, supports video + data + power</td></tr>
          <tr><td>VGA</td><td>Analog, only video, D-Sub 15-pin connector — legacy, being phased out</td></tr>
          <tr><td>DVI</td><td>Can be analog/digital/both. DVI-D (digital only), DVI-I (integrated analog+digital)</td></tr>
        </table>

        <h3>Mobile Device Types</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Smartphones:</strong> Android & iOS — closed vs. open ecosystem</li>
          <li><strong>Tablets:</strong> iPad (iOS/iPadOS), Android tablets</li>
          <li><strong>Wearables:</strong> Smartwatches, fitness trackers</li>
          <li><strong>E-readers:</strong> E-Ink display — easy on eyes, long battery</li>
          <li><strong>GPS:</strong> Uses satellites (no cellular needed for location)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Mobile Wireless Technologies</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Technology</th><th>Range / Speed</th><th>Use Case</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Bluetooth</td><td>~10m / up to 3 Mbps</td><td>Headsets, keyboards, peripherals</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wi-Fi (802.11)</td><td>~50m+ / up to 9.6 Gbps (ax)</td><td>General wireless internet</td></tr>
          <tr><td>NFC</td><td>~4cm / slow</td><td>Tap-to-pay, pairing</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Infrared (IR)</td><td>Short / slow</td><td>TV remotes, old phone transfer</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cellular (4G/5G)</td><td>Miles / up to 20 Gbps</td><td>Mobile internet</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GPS</td><td>Global / N/A</td><td>Navigation (receive-only)</td></tr>
        </table>

        <div class="must-know">
          <div class="must-know-header">⚠️ MUST KNOW — Mobile Connections</div>
          <p>• <strong>Lightning:</strong> Apple proprietary (older iPhones/iPads)</p>
          <p>• <strong>USB-C:</strong> New standard — reversible, supports data/video/power. iPhone 15+ uses USB-C</p>
          <p>• <strong>Micro-USB:</strong> Older Android devices</p>
          <p>• <strong>Hotspot/Tethering:</strong> Sharing cellular data with other devices via Wi-Fi, USB, or Bluetooth</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Mobile Device Synchronization</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>iCloud:</strong> Apple's cloud sync service — contacts, photos, docs</li>
          <li><strong>Google Workspace:</strong> Android/cross-platform sync</li>
          <li><strong>Microsoft 365:</strong> Cross-platform, enterprise-focused</li>
          <li><strong>ActiveSync:</strong> Microsoft protocol for syncing email/calendar/contacts to mobile</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- NETWORKING -->
      <div class="content-card" id="c1-networking">
        <h2><div class="section-icon icon-purple">🌐</div> Domain 2: Networking (20%)</h2>
        <p>The highest-value conceptual domain. Know protocols, ports, cables, wireless, and network devices cold.</p>

        <div class="must-know">
          <div class="must-know-header">⚠️ MUST KNOW — TCP/IP Ports (memorize these!)</div>
          <p>• <strong>20/21</strong> — FTP (20=data, 21=control) — File Transfer Protocol</p>
          <p>• <strong>22</strong> — SSH — Secure Shell (encrypted remote access)</p>
          <p>• <strong>23</strong> — Telnet — Unencrypted remote access (insecure!)</p>
          <p>• <strong>25</strong> — SMTP — Sending email</p>
          <p>• <strong>53</strong> — DNS — Domain Name System (resolves names to IPs)</p>
          <p>• <strong>67/68</strong> — DHCP — 67=server, 68=client (assigns IP addresses)</p>
          <p>• <strong>80</strong> — HTTP — Unencrypted web</p>
          <p>• <strong>110</strong> — POP3 — Download email (deletes from server)</p>
          <p>• <strong>143</strong> — IMAP — Email stays on server, sync multiple devices</p>
          <p>• <strong>443</strong> — HTTPS — Encrypted web (TLS)</p>
          <p>• <strong>3389</strong> — RDP — Remote Desktop Protocol (Windows)</p>
          <p>• <strong>137-139</strong> — NetBIOS — Windows file sharing</p>
          <p>• <strong>445</strong> — SMB — Server Message Block (Windows file/print sharing)</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Network Devices</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Device</th><th>OSI Layer</th><th>Function</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Hub</td><td>Layer 1 (Physical)</td><td>Broadcasts all traffic to all ports — dumb, causes collisions</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Switch</td><td>Layer 2 (Data Link)</td><td>Uses MAC addresses to direct traffic only to correct port</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Router</td><td>Layer 3 (Network)</td><td>Routes between networks using IP addresses, connects to internet</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Access Point</td><td>Layer 2</td><td>Extends wireless network; provides Wi-Fi connectivity</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Firewall</td><td>Layer 3–7</td><td>Filters traffic by rules; blocks unwanted connections</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Modem</td><td>Layer 1–2</td><td>Converts ISP signal (coax/DSL) to Ethernet</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PoE Switch</td><td>Layer 2</td><td>Power over Ethernet — powers cameras, APs via cable</td></tr>
        </table>

        <h3>Ethernet Cabling Standards</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Category</th><th>Max Speed</th><th>Max Distance</th><th>Use</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Cat 5</td><td>100 Mbps</td><td>100m</td><td>Legacy — avoid</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cat 5e</td><td>1 Gbps</td><td>100m</td><td>Common home/office</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cat 6</td><td>10 Gbps</td><td>55m (10G) / 100m (1G)</td><td>Modern standard</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cat 6a</td><td>10 Gbps</td><td>100m</td><td>Data centers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cat 7</td><td>10 Gbps</td><td>100m</td><td>Shielded, specialized</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cat 8</td><td>25–40 Gbps</td><td>30m</td><td>Data center short runs</td></tr>
        </table>

        <div class="must-know">
          <div class="must-know-header">⚠️ MUST KNOW — Cable Types</div>
          <p>• <strong>UTP</strong> — Unshielded Twisted Pair — most common, no shielding</p>
          <p>• <strong>STP</strong> — Shielded Twisted Pair — better EMI protection, more expensive</p>
          <p>• <strong>Coax</strong> — RG-6 for cable TV/internet; RG-59 for older systems</p>
          <p>• <strong>Fiber Optic</strong> — Light signals, immune to EMI, very long runs</p>
          <p>• <strong>Single-mode fiber (SMF)</strong> — Yellow jacket, long distance (km), laser</p>
          <p>• <strong>Multi-mode fiber (MMF)</strong> — Orange/aqua jacket, shorter distance, LED</p>
          <p>• <strong>Plenum-rated cable</strong> — Fire-resistant, required in air ducts/ceilings</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Wi-Fi Standards (802.11)</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Standard</th><th>Name</th><th>Max Speed</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
          <tr><td>802.11a</td><td>Wi-Fi 1</td><td>54 Mbps</td><td>5 GHz</td></tr>
          <tr><td>802.11b</td><td>Wi-Fi 2</td><td>11 Mbps</td><td>2.4 GHz</td></tr>
          <tr><td>802.11g</td><td>Wi-Fi 3</td><td>54 Mbps</td><td>2.4 GHz</td></tr>
          <tr><td>802.11n</td><td>Wi-Fi 4</td><td>600 Mbps</td><td>2.4 & 5 GHz</td></tr>
          <tr><td>802.11ac</td><td>Wi-Fi 5</td><td>3.5 Gbps</td><td>5 GHz</td></tr>
          <tr><td>802.11ax</td><td>Wi-Fi 6/6E</td><td>9.6 Gbps</td><td>2.4, 5, 6 GHz</td></tr>
        </table>

        <div class="mnemonic">
          <div class="mnemonic-label">🧠 MNEMONIC — 802.11 Order</div>
          <div class="mnemonic-word">"A Boy Going Nowhere Across Airports"</div>
          <p><b>A</b> (a) <b>B</b>oy (b) <b>G</b>oing (g) <b>N</b>owhere (n) <b>A</b>cross (ac) <b>A</b>irports (ax)</p>
        </div>

        <h3>IP Addressing Fundamentals</h3>
        <div class="must-know">
          <div class="must-know-header">⚠️ MUST KNOW — IPv4 Private Ranges</div>
          <p>• <strong>10.0.0.0 – 10.255.255.255</strong> — Class A (/8) — Large organizations</p>
          <p>• <strong>172.16.0.0 – 172.31.255.255</strong> — Class B (/12) — Medium organizations</p>
          <p>• <strong>192.168.0.0 – 192.168.255.255</strong> — Class C (/16) — Home/small networks</p>
          <p>• <strong>169.254.x.x</strong> — APIPA — Assigned when DHCP fails (link-local)</p>
          <p>• <strong>127.0.0.1</strong> — Loopback (localhost) — always refers to this computer</p>
        </div>

        <p>Key subnetting: <code>/24</code> = 255.255.255.0 = 256 addresses (254 usable). <code>/16</code> = 255.255.0.0. <code>/8</code> = 255.0.0.0</p>

        <h3>Network Types</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
          <tr><td>LAN</td><td>Local Area Network — same building/campus</td></tr>
          <tr><td>WAN</td><td>Wide Area Network — connects LANs across distance (Internet = biggest WAN)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>MAN</td><td>Metropolitan Area Network — city-wide</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PAN</td><td>Personal Area Network — Bluetooth, very short range</td></tr>
          <tr><td>WLAN</td><td>Wireless LAN</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SAN</td><td>Storage Area Network — high-speed storage network</td></tr>
          <tr><td>VLAN</td><td>Virtual LAN — logical segmentation on same physical switch</td></tr>
        </table>

        <h3>Network Tools & Commands</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
          <tr><td>ping</td><td>Tests connectivity to a host (uses ICMP)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ipconfig / ifconfig</td><td>Shows IP configuration (Windows / Linux/Mac)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>tracert / traceroute</td><td>Shows path packets take to destination</td></tr>
          <tr><td>nslookup</td><td>Queries DNS to resolve hostnames</td></tr>
          <tr><td>netstat</td><td>Shows active network connections and ports</td></tr>
          <tr><td>nmap</td><td>Port scanner — discovers hosts and services</td></tr>
          <tr><td>arp</td><td>Shows/manages ARP table (IP-to-MAC mappings)</td></tr>
        </table>
      </div>

      <!-- HARDWARE -->
      <div class="content-card" id="c1-hardware">
        <h2><div class="section-icon icon-green">🖥️</div> Domain 3: Hardware (25%)</h2>
        <p>The biggest domain — know your components, connectors, storage, and peripherals inside and out.</p>

        <h3>Motherboard Components</h3>
        <div class="must-know">
          <div class="must-know-header">⚠️ MUST KNOW — Motherboard Facts</div>
          <p>• <strong>Form factors:</strong> ATX (full size) > Micro-ATX > Mini-ITX (smallest)</p>
          <p>• <strong>CPU socket types:</strong> Intel uses LGA (pins on motherboard), AMD uses AM4/AM5 (pins on CPU)</p>
          <p>• <strong>BIOS/UEFI:</strong> Firmware that initializes hardware. UEFI is modern replacement for BIOS</p>
          <p>• <strong>CMOS battery:</strong> CR2032 coin cell — keeps BIOS settings and clock when powered off</p>
          <p>• <strong>Chipset:</strong> Controls communication between CPU, RAM, PCIe, storage</p>
          <p>• <strong>PCIe lanes:</strong> x1 (1 lane), x4, x8, x16 (GPU slot — 16 lanes)</td></p>
        </div>

        <h3>RAM (Memory)</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Type</th><th>Speed</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
          <tr><td>DDR3</td><td>800–2133 MHz</td><td>Legacy — older systems</td></tr>
          <tr><td>DDR4</td><td>2133–5000 MHz</td><td>Current mainstream standard</td></tr>
          <tr><td>DDR5</td><td>4800+ MHz</td><td>Newest — faster, more efficient</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ECC RAM</td><td>Any</td><td>Error-Correcting Code — used in servers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SO-DIMM</td><td>Any</td><td>Laptop form factor — smaller</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p><strong>Channels:</strong> Dual-channel = 2 sticks in matching slots = double bandwidth. Always install in pairs for dual-channel.</p>

        <h3>Storage Devices</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Type</th><th>Interface</th><th>Speed</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
          <tr><td>HDD</td><td>SATA</td><td>100–200 MB/s</td><td>Spinning platter, cheap per GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SATA SSD</td><td>SATA III</td><td>~550 MB/s</td><td>2.5" or M.2 SATA</td></tr>
          <tr><td>M.2 NVMe</td><td>PCIe 3.0/4.0</td><td>3,500–7,000 MB/s</td><td>Fastest, small M.2 form factor</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Optical (DVD)</td><td>SATA</td><td>~1 MB/s</td><td>CD/DVD/Blu-ray</td></tr>
          <tr><td>USB Flash</td><td>USB</td><td>Varies</td><td>Portable storage</td></tr>
        </table>

        <div class="must-know">
          <div class="must-know-header">⚠️ MUST KNOW — RAID Types</div>
          <p>• <strong>RAID 0</strong> — Striping, NO redundancy, fastest read/write. Lose 1 drive = lose all data</p>
          <p>• <strong>RAID 1</strong> — Mirroring, full redundancy, 50% usable space. Lose 1 drive = safe</p>
          <p>• <strong>RAID 5</strong> — Striping + parity, min 3 drives, can lose 1 drive</p>
          <p>• <strong>RAID 6</strong> — Like RAID 5 but can lose 2 drives, min 4 drives</p>
          <p>• <strong>RAID 10</strong> — Mirror + stripe, min 4 drives, high performance + redundancy</p>
          <p>Remember: RAID is NOT a backup!</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Power Supply (PSU)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Wattage:</strong> Must meet total system demand + 20% headroom</li>
          <li><strong>80 Plus rating:</strong> Bronze → Silver → Gold → Platinum → Titanium (efficiency)</li>
          <li><strong>Connectors:</strong> 24-pin ATX (motherboard), 8-pin EPS (CPU), PCIe 6/8-pin (GPU)</li>
          <li><strong>Modular PSU:</strong> Only cable in cables you need — cleaner builds</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Expansion Cards</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Card</th><th>Function</th></tr>
          <tr><td>GPU (Video Card)</td><td>Renders graphics — uses PCIe x16 slot</td></tr>
          <tr><td>NIC (Network Card)</td><td>Adds network port — PCIe x1 or USB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sound Card</td><td>Audio I/O — PCIe x1 or USB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>RAID Controller</td><td>Hardware RAID management</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Capture Card</td><td>Records video from external sources</td></tr>
        </table>

        <h3>Connectors & Ports (Must Know)</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Port</th><th>Shape/Pins</th><th>Key Fact</th></tr>
          <tr><td>USB-A</td><td>Rectangular</td><td>Original USB — directional</td></tr>
          <tr><td>USB-C</td><td>Oval, reversible</td><td>Modern standard — supports USB3, Thunderbolt, video</td></tr>
          <tr><td>USB 3.0</td><td>Blue color</td><td>5 Gbps — blue ports/plugs</td></tr>
          <tr><td>USB 3.1</td><td>Teal/blue</td><td>10 Gbps</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Thunderbolt 3/4</td><td>USB-C + lightning bolt</td><td>40 Gbps, supports 2x4K monitors</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SATA Data</td><td>L-shaped 7-pin</td><td>Connects HDD/SSD to motherboard</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SATA Power</td><td>15-pin flat</td><td>Power from PSU to drives</td></tr>
        </table>

        <h3>Cooling Systems</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Air cooling:</strong> Heatsink + fan — attaches to CPU, standard approach</li>
          <li><strong>Liquid/AIO cooling:</strong> All-in-one cooler — pump, radiator, fans. Better for overclocking</li>
          <li><strong>Thermal paste:</strong> Applied between CPU and heatsink — improves heat transfer</li>
          <li><strong>Case fans:</strong> Intake (front/bottom) and exhaust (rear/top) — positive pressure vs. negative</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- VIRTUALIZATION & CLOUD -->
      <div class="content-card" id="c1-virt">
        <h2><div class="section-icon icon-yellow">☁️</div> Domain 4: Virtualization & Cloud (11%)</h2>

        <h3>Virtualization Concepts</h3>
        <div class="must-know">
          <div class="must-know-header">⚠️ MUST KNOW — Key Terms</div>
          <p>• <strong>Hypervisor:</strong> Software that creates and manages VMs</p>
          <p>• <strong>Type 1 Hypervisor:</strong> Bare-metal — runs directly on hardware. Examples: VMware ESXi, Hyper-V (server), Proxmox</p>
          <p>• <strong>Type 2 Hypervisor:</strong> Hosted — runs on top of an OS. Examples: VMware Workstation, VirtualBox</p>
          <p>• <strong>VM (Virtual Machine):</strong> Emulates a complete computer in software</p>
          <p>• <strong>Container:</strong> Lightweight virtualization — shares host OS kernel (Docker)</p>
          <p>• <strong>Snapshot:</strong> Point-in-time save of VM state — easy rollback</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Cloud Service Models</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Model</th><th>What You Manage</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
          <tr><td>IaaS — Infrastructure</td><td>OS, apps, data</td><td>AWS EC2, Azure VMs, Google Compute</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PaaS — Platform</td><td>Apps, data only</td><td>Heroku, Azure App Service, Google App Engine</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SaaS — Software</td><td>Just use it</td><td>Gmail, Office 365, Salesforce, Dropbox</td></tr>
        </table>

        <div class="mnemonic">
          <div class="mnemonic-label">🧠 MNEMONIC — Cloud Models (bottom to top)</div>
          <div class="mnemonic-word">"I Picked Software"</div>
          <p><b>I</b>aaS — <b>P</b>aaS — <b>S</b>aaS (more managed each step up)</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Cloud Deployment Models</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Public Cloud:</strong> Shared infrastructure (AWS, Azure, GCP) — pay-as-you-go</li>
          <li><strong>Private Cloud:</strong> Dedicated infrastructure — organization owns/controls</li>
          <li><strong>Hybrid Cloud:</strong> Mix of public + private — flexible</li>
          <li><strong>Community Cloud:</strong> Shared by specific group (e.g., healthcare orgs)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Cloud Characteristics</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>On-demand self-service:</strong> Provision resources without human interaction</li>
          <li><strong>Rapid elasticity:</strong> Scale up/down automatically</li>
          <li><strong>Measured service:</strong> Pay only for what you use</li>
          <li><strong>Resource pooling:</strong> Shared resources across multiple customers (multi-tenancy)</li>
          <li><strong>Broad network access:</strong> Accessible from anywhere</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- HARDWARE TROUBLESHOOTING -->
      <div class="content-card" id="c1-troubleshoot">
        <h2><div class="section-icon icon-blue">🔧</div> Domain 5: Hardware Troubleshooting (29%)</h2>
        <p>The largest domain by weight. Follow the <strong>CompTIA troubleshooting methodology</strong> and know common symptoms.</p>

        <div class="must-know">
          <div class="must-know-header">⚠️ MUST KNOW — CompTIA 6-Step Troubleshooting Process</div>
          <p>1. <strong>Identify the problem</strong> — gather info, question the user, check logs</p>
          <p>2. <strong>Establish a theory of probable cause</strong> — consider obvious causes first (Occam's Razor)</p>
          <p>3. <strong>Test the theory</strong> — confirm it's right; if wrong, re-establish new theory</p>
          <p>4. <strong>Establish a plan of action</strong> — make a plan, identify side effects</p>
          <p>5. <strong>Implement the solution</strong> — fix it or escalate if needed</p>
          <p>6. <strong>Verify functionality & prevent recurrence</strong> — document, educate user</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Common Hardware Symptoms</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely Cause</th></tr>
          <tr><td>POST beep codes</td><td>Hardware failure — beeps indicate specific errors (RAM, GPU, CPU)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No POST / no power</td><td>Dead PSU, loose power cable, bad motherboard</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Blue Screen of Death (BSOD)</td><td>Driver issue, bad RAM, overheating, failing HDD</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Grinding noise from PC</td><td>Failing HDD (clicking) or failing fan bearing</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Overheating / shutdowns</td><td>Clogged fans, bad thermal paste, broken cooler</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Artifacts on screen</td><td>Failing GPU or overheating GPU</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Slow performance</td><td>HDD failing, insufficient RAM, malware, overheating</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No display but power on</td><td>Bad GPU, loose cable, bad monitor</td></tr>
        </table>

        <h3>Printer Types & Troubleshooting</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Printer Type</th><th>How It Works</th><th>Key Issues</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Laser</td><td>Toner + heat/pressure fuser</td><td>Fuser failure (smeared toner), drum issues</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Inkjet</td><td>Liquid ink sprayed</td><td>Clogged heads, banding, smears</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Thermal</td><td>Heat-sensitive paper</td><td>Faded output = bad paper or head, no toner needed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Impact/Dot Matrix</td><td>Pins strike ribbon</td><td>Faded = worn ribbon, used for multi-part forms</td></tr>
        </table>

        <div class="must-know">
          <div class="must-know-header">⚠️ MUST KNOW — Laser Printer Process Order</div>
          <p>Remember: <strong>Processing, Charging, Exposing, Developing, Transferring, Fusing, Cleaning</strong></p>
          <p>Mnemonic: <strong>"Please Come Early, Do The Fun Class"</strong></p>
        </div>

        <h3>Display Troubleshooting</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Dead pixels:</strong> Always black — hardware defect</li>
          <li><strong>Stuck pixels:</strong> Always one color — can sometimes be fixed with software</li>
          <li><strong>Backlight bleeding:</strong> Light leaks at edges — normal for IPS panels</li>
          <li><strong>Burn-in:</strong> Ghost image burned in — common on OLED/plasma</li>
          <li><strong>Flickering:</strong> Bad cable, bad GPU connection, or refresh rate mismatch</li>
          <li><strong>Dim display:</strong> Backlight failure or brightness setting too low</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

