        <h2><div class="section-icon icon-green">💻</div> Domain 1: Operating Systems (31%)</h2>
        <p>Biggest domain. Know Windows inside and out, plus macOS and Linux basics.</p>

        <h3>Windows Editions</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Edition</th><th>Use Case</th><th>Key Feature</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Windows Home</td><td>Consumers</td><td>Basic features, no Group Policy, no domain join</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Windows Pro</td><td>Business/power users</td><td>Group Policy, BitLocker, Remote Desktop (host), domain join</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Windows Pro for Workstations</td><td>High-end workstations</td><td>NVMe RAID, ReFS file system, more RAM support</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Windows Enterprise</td><td>Large organizations</td><td>DirectAccess, AppLocker, BranchCache</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Windows Education</td><td>Schools</td><td>Similar to Enterprise</td></tr>
        </table>

        <div class="must-know">
          <div class="must-know-header">⚠️ MUST KNOW — Windows Key Tools</div>
          <p>• <strong>Task Manager</strong> — Ctrl+Shift+Esc — see processes, CPU/RAM usage, kill tasks</p>
          <p>• <strong>Device Manager</strong> — devmgmt.msc — driver issues, hardware conflicts (yellow !)</p>
          <p>• <strong>Event Viewer</strong> — eventvwr.msc — log of all system events and errors</p>
          <p>• <strong>Registry Editor</strong> — regedit — edit Windows registry (dangerous!)</p>
          <p>• <strong>Group Policy</strong> — gpedit.msc — Pro/Enterprise only — enforce settings</p>
          <p>• <strong>Computer Management</strong> — compmgmt.msc — all-in-one admin console</p>
          <p>• <strong>Disk Management</strong> — diskmgmt.msc — partition/format drives</p>
          <p>• <strong>Services</strong> — services.msc — start/stop/configure background services</p>
          <p>• <strong>System Configuration</strong> — msconfig — startup, boot options, safe mode</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Windows File Systems</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>File System</th><th>Use</th><th>Key Facts</th></tr>
          <tr><td>NTFS</td><td>Windows default</td><td>Permissions, encryption, compression, journaling, large files</td></tr>
          <tr><td>FAT32</td><td>USB drives, compatibility</td><td>Max file size 4GB, max volume 32GB, compatible with everything</td></tr>
          <tr><td>exFAT</td><td>Modern USB/SD</td><td>No file size limit, good cross-platform compatibility</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ReFS</td><td>Windows Server/Workstations Pro</td><td>Resilient File System — self-healing, large volumes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ext4</td><td>Linux</td><td>Default Linux file system</td></tr>
          <tr><td>APFS</td><td>macOS</td><td>Apple File System — SSD optimized, encryption</td></tr>
          <tr><td>HFS+</td><td>Older macOS</td><td>Legacy — replaced by APFS</td></tr>
        </table>

        <h3>Windows Command Line Tools</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tr><th>Command</th><th>Function</th></tr>
          <tr><td>sfc /scannow</td><td>System File Checker — fixes corrupted Windows files</td></tr>
          <tr><td>DISM</td><td>Deployment Image Servicing — repairs Windows image</td></tr>
          <tr><td>chkdsk</td><td>Check disk for errors and bad sectors</td></tr>
          <tr><td>diskpart</td><td>Advanced disk partitioning tool</td></tr>
          <tr><td>format</td><td>Formats a drive</td></tr>
          <tr><td>net use</td><td>Map/disconnect network drives</td></tr>
          <tr><td>gpupdate /force</td><td>Forces Group Policy refresh</td></tr>
          <tr><td>shutdown /r /t 0</td><td>Immediate restart</td></tr>
          <tr><td>robocopy</td><td>Robust file copy with advanced options</td></tr>
          <tr><td>xcopy</td><td>Extended copy — copies files and directories</td></tr>
        </table>

        <h3>Boot Process & Startup</h3>
        <div class="must-know">
          <div class="must-know-header">⚠️ MUST KNOW — Windows Boot Process</div>
          <p>1. <strong>POST</strong> — Power-On Self Test (hardware check)</p>
          <p>2. <strong>BIOS/UEFI</strong> — Finds boot device</p>
          <p>3. <strong>MBR or GPT</strong> — Partition table (MBR = legacy, GPT = modern UEFI)</p>
          <p>4. <strong>Boot Manager (bootmgr)</strong> — Loads Windows boot loader</p>
          <p>5. <strong>winload.exe</strong> — Loads Windows kernel</p>
          <p>6. <strong>ntoskrnl.exe</strong> — Windows kernel loads drivers, starts services</p>
          <p>7. <strong>winlogon.exe / LogonUI</strong> — Login screen appears</p>
        </div>

